import @CALCULUS@.Diagnostics

/-!
The `@EXE@` executable: read a proof in the `@CALCULUS@` calculus and report
whether it is a refutation.

Reading the file aside, the work belongs in `@CALCULUS@.Api`: parse the text,
then run the checks that stand for the hypotheses of the soundness theorem in
`@CALCULUS@/ApiCorrect.lean`. This file only reads the file, prints the verdict
and picks an exit status, so that what the theorem is stated about and what the
executable does are the same function.

The three verdicts, following Logos:

| output       | status | meaning                                              |
| ------------ | ------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `correct`    | 0      | the proof's assumptions are unsatisfiable            |
| `incorrect`  | 1      | the proof is not accepted as a refutation            |
| `incomplete` | 2      | accepted, but it mentions something the semantics does not model, so the theorem does not apply |

`incomplete` is the verdict that keeps the theorem honest: the side conditions
are checked at run time, and a proof that passes the checker but fails them is
reported as such rather than as `correct`.

No calculus is compiled in yet, so no verdict can be reached. What is still
missing, and in what order it is worth building, is the Eudaimonia roadmap in
its TODO.md.
-/

def usage : String :=
  s!"usage: @EXE@ <proof>\n\n\
     Check a proof in the {@CALCULUS@.calculusName} calculus."

def main : List String → IO UInt32
  | [path] => do
    let _input ← IO.FS.readFile path
    IO.eprintln s!"@EXE@: no calculus is installed yet, so {path} cannot be checked."
    IO.eprintln "Compile a Eunoia signature into signature/ to replace the stubs \
                 under @CALCULUS@/."
    return 1
  | _ => do
    IO.eprintln usage
    return 1
